      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 American Academy of Allergy, Asthma & Immunology. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Corticosteroid (CS) treatment has been established as the first anti-inflammatory treatment for adults and children with asthma. However, a subset of patients fails to respond to combined systemic and inhaled CS treatment."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "This study was aimed at further understanding CS resistance among children with severe asthma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "High-resolution metabolomics was performed on urine samples from CS-respondent (n = 15) and CS-nonrespondent (n = 15) children to determine possible urine biomarkers related to CS resistance. The metabolic phenotypes of CS responders and CS nonresponders were analyzed using bioinformatics including Manhattan plot with false- discovery rate, hierarchical cluster analysis, Kyoto Encyclopedia Genes and Genomes, and Mummichog pathway analysis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The 2-way hierarchical cluster analysis study determined 30 metabolites showing significantly different levels between CS responders and CS nonresponders. The important metabolites annotated were 3,6-dihydronicotinic acid (126.05 m/z, RT: 106, [M+H](+)), 3-methoxy-4-hydroxyphenyl(ethylene)glycol (185.05 m/z, RT: 155, [M+H](+)), 3,4-dihydroxy-phenylalanine (198.07 m/z, RT: 446, [M+H](+)), γ-glutamylcysteine (236.06 m/z, RT: 528, [M+S(34)+H](+)), Cys-Gly, (253.06 m/z, RT: 528, [M-NH3+H](+)), and reduced Flavin mononucleotide (517.0794 m/z, RT: 533, [M+NaCl](+)). Tyrosine metabolism, degradation of aromatic compounds, and glutathione metabolism are suggested to be significant pathways relating to CS resistance."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "High-resolution metabolomics is a promising approach in asthma research. Five candidate markers were identified to be related to CS-resistant children with severe asthma. These compounds, upon validation, may contribute further in the understanding of CS resistance among children with severe asthma through the use of urine."
          }
        ]
      },
